WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
Four samples, all needing an acid-base analysis: a

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.040
bottle of white vinegar, an antacid tablet, a

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.520
glass of red wine, and the water flowing

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.080
out of a factory pipe.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.680
You've now met the methods you need: a

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.200
pH probe, a direct titration, a back titration

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.440
and a conductivity titration.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.240
But which one fits each sample, and what

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.320
does each measurement actually tell you?

00:00:25.534 --> 00:00:27.373
Start with what you need to know.

00:00:27.854 --> 00:00:30.253
Sometimes it's how acidic the sample is right

00:00:30.334 --> 00:00:31.693
now, its pH.

00:00:32.174 --> 00:00:35.133
A calibrated pH probe reads that in seconds.

00:00:35.694 --> 00:00:37.933
Other times it's how much acid or base

00:00:38.014 --> 00:00:38.973
is there in total.

00:00:39.454 --> 00:00:40.733
That needs a titration.

00:00:41.294 --> 00:00:43.773
As in the titration video, a weak acid's

00:00:43.854 --> 00:00:46.413
pH reflects only the small fraction that has

00:00:46.494 --> 00:00:50.613
ionised, but a titration reacts every acid molecule.

00:00:50.894 --> 00:00:53.213
That's why vinegar at pH two point four

00:00:53.614 --> 00:00:56.013
still holds about zero point eight eight moles

00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:58.013
per litre of ethanoic acid.

00:00:59.234 --> 00:01:01.793
A pH probe is a digital instrument with

00:01:01.874 --> 00:01:04.193
a thin glass bulb at its tip.

00:01:04.274 --> 00:01:07.073
Hydronium ions interact with the outer surface of

00:01:07.114 --> 00:01:09.313
the glass, and that sets up a small

00:01:09.394 --> 00:01:10.993
voltage across it.

00:01:11.034 --> 00:01:13.473
The meter measures the voltage and converts it

00:01:13.554 --> 00:01:14.833
into a pH.

00:01:15.154 --> 00:01:18.193
At twenty five degrees, each pH unit changes

00:01:18.274 --> 00:01:20.833
the voltage by about fifty nine millivolts.

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:23.153
And because it reads a voltage, not a

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:26.353
colour, a dark or cloudy sample doesn't hide

00:01:26.434 --> 00:01:27.153
the reading.

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:30.206
But the meter has to be told which

00:01:30.287 --> 00:01:33.486
voltage means which pH, and that drifts as

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.366
the glass ages and as the temperature changes.

00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:39.006
So you calibrate it with two buffers of

00:01:39.087 --> 00:01:40.366
known pH.

00:01:40.687 --> 00:01:43.486
A pH seven buffer sets the zero point,

00:01:43.527 --> 00:01:45.246
the reading at pH seven.

00:01:45.567 --> 00:01:48.926
A second buffer, pH four for acidic samples,

00:01:49.007 --> 00:01:52.406
sets the slope, the millivolts per pH unit.

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:55.646
Choose buffers close to your samples, ideally on

00:01:55.727 --> 00:01:58.366
either side, and rinse the probe between them.

00:01:58.847 --> 00:02:00.606
Skip it, and the probe can show a

00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:02.766
neat number that's simply wrong.

00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:07.086
Probes also do jobs a titration can't.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:09.486
At a treatment plant, a probe sits in

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:11.966
the outlet pipe and logs the pH every

00:02:12.047 --> 00:02:14.766
few seconds, and a controller adds acid or

00:02:14.847 --> 00:02:17.286
base before the pH can drift outside the

00:02:17.327 --> 00:02:19.406
range on the plant's licence.

00:02:19.487 --> 00:02:21.646
And in many labs, a probe follows a

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:26.326
titration automatically: an autotitrator adds base, records the

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:29.326
pH curve and finds the jump itself.

00:02:29.407 --> 00:02:31.406
No one has to judge a colour change.

00:02:32.634 --> 00:02:34.353
If you need an amount, look at the

00:02:34.394 --> 00:02:35.113
sample.

00:02:35.354 --> 00:02:37.753
Does it dissolve and react quickly, and is

00:02:37.834 --> 00:02:38.393
it clear?

00:02:38.874 --> 00:02:41.753
Then titrate it directly, with an indicator, like

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.433
vinegar with phenolphthalein.

00:02:43.674 --> 00:02:46.633
Is it an insoluble or slow reacting solid,

00:02:46.714 --> 00:02:47.913
like the antacid?

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:49.113
Then back titrate.

00:02:49.514 --> 00:02:51.913
Is it coloured or cloudy, so an indicator

00:02:51.994 --> 00:02:52.913
can't be seen?

00:02:53.114 --> 00:02:55.993
Then follow the titration with a meter instead:

00:02:56.074 --> 00:02:58.153
a pH probe to find the jump, or

00:02:58.207 --> 00:03:00.393
a conductivity meter to find where the lines

00:03:00.474 --> 00:03:01.273
cross.

00:03:01.394 --> 00:03:03.273
And with a soft drink, boil off the

00:03:03.354 --> 00:03:06.473
carbon dioxide first, or it titrates as carbonic

00:03:06.554 --> 00:03:07.433
acid too.

00:03:08.634 --> 00:03:10.153
Try it on the red wine.

00:03:10.394 --> 00:03:12.913
A winemaker wants its total acid.

00:03:13.114 --> 00:03:15.513
That's an amount, so it's a titration.

00:03:15.994 --> 00:03:19.033
Wine reacts quickly with sodium hydroxide, but it's

00:03:19.114 --> 00:03:21.993
dark red, so phenolphthalein's pink would be hard

00:03:22.074 --> 00:03:22.553
to see.

00:03:23.114 --> 00:03:25.753
First remove dissolved carbon dioxide.

00:03:25.914 --> 00:03:29.033
Then titrate the wine with standard sodium hydroxide

00:03:29.074 --> 00:03:31.673
to the stated pH endpoint, read with a

00:03:31.754 --> 00:03:33.593
calibrated pH probe.

00:03:33.674 --> 00:03:36.553
The titre gives the wine's titratable acidity to

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:39.113
that endpoint, not the amount of each acid

00:03:39.194 --> 00:03:40.233
separately.

00:03:40.274 --> 00:03:43.513
Tartaric, malic and the rest are counted together,

00:03:43.554 --> 00:03:45.593
and it doesn't give the wine's pH either.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:50.080
Acid-base analysis isn't only done with glassware.

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.760
The syllabus also asks about acid-base techniques used

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.480
by Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Peoples.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.760
Their chemical knowledge has been built by observing,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.560
testing and passing on the results over many

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.280
generations, and it's still held today.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.080
It belongs to the communities who hold it.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.120
The cycad leaching in Module 5 is one

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:14.880
documented example, though its chemistry is solubility.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.840
Where a practice uses acid-base chemistry, it rests

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.520
on the idea from this module: a base

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.560
takes protons from an acid, and that changes

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:26.160
how a substance behaves.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.726
So here's what to remember.

00:04:29.287 --> 00:04:32.886
pH tells you how acidic; a titration tells

00:04:32.967 --> 00:04:33.846
you how much.

00:04:34.407 --> 00:04:36.766
A probe senses only the hydronium in the

00:04:36.807 --> 00:04:37.926
solution now.

00:04:38.327 --> 00:04:42.006
A titration reacts every acid molecule, ionised or

00:04:42.087 --> 00:04:42.726
not.

00:04:43.047 --> 00:04:45.366
For a strong acid, the pH does give

00:04:45.447 --> 00:04:46.646
the concentration.

00:04:46.727 --> 00:04:48.886
For a weak acid, it doesn't.

00:04:48.967 --> 00:04:50.966
Then let the sample decide which kind of

00:04:51.047 --> 00:04:51.846
titration.

00:04:53.067 --> 00:04:53.706
Your turn.

00:04:53.987 --> 00:04:56.826
A garden supplier wants the percentage of calcium

00:04:56.987 --> 00:04:58.906
carbonate in crushed limestone.

00:04:59.387 --> 00:05:02.266
A technician suggests mixing the limestone with water

00:05:02.587 --> 00:05:05.346
and reading the pH with a calibrated probe.

00:05:05.627 --> 00:05:08.586
Explain why this won't give the percentage, then

00:05:08.907 --> 00:05:11.906
recommend a more suitable method and justify it.

00:05:12.187 --> 00:05:13.546
Pause here to answer.

00:05:23.967 --> 00:05:27.566
Calcium carbonate barely dissolves, so the pH shows

00:05:27.647 --> 00:05:30.606
only the tiny amount in solution, and a

00:05:30.687 --> 00:05:32.526
pH isn't an amount anyway.

00:05:33.167 --> 00:05:36.126
Use a back titration: add a measured excess

00:05:36.167 --> 00:05:39.566
of standard acid, let the solid react completely,

00:05:39.687 --> 00:05:41.166
then titrate the leftover.

00:05:41.727 --> 00:05:43.726
Half the acid used gives the moles of

00:05:43.807 --> 00:05:45.086
calcium carbonate.

00:05:45.407 --> 00:05:48.446
A direct titration is the trap: the solid

00:05:48.607 --> 00:05:51.086
reacts too slowly for a clean endpoint.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:52.940
Last one.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:56.780
Aspirin is a monoprotic acid, and a pharmacist

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.820
checks tablets by titrating them with standard sodium

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:00.700
hydroxide.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.060
In old tablets, some aspirin has reacted with

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:07.820
water: each molecule forms one molecule of ethanoic

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:11.180
acid and one of salicylic acid, both acting

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:13.020
as monoprotic acids.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:15.740
Would the titration give an aspirin content that's

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.580
too high or too low?

00:06:17.980 --> 00:06:18.620
Explain.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:20.540
Pause here to answer.

00:06:30.967 --> 00:06:31.766
Too high.

00:06:32.247 --> 00:06:34.966
Each aspirin molecule that broke down now gives

00:06:35.127 --> 00:06:38.006
two acid molecules, and each one reacts with

00:06:38.087 --> 00:06:40.806
a hydroxide, so the titre is bigger than

00:06:40.847 --> 00:06:42.686
the aspirin alone would need.

00:06:42.967 --> 00:06:46.086
The titration counts total acid; it can't tell

00:06:46.167 --> 00:06:47.526
which acid it came from.

00:06:48.247 --> 00:06:51.606
Saying too low, because there's less aspirin, misses

00:06:51.687 --> 00:06:53.286
that the acid hasn't gone.

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:57.540
So for any sample, ask two questions.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.260
Do I need its pH, or an amount?

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:03.300
For pH, use a calibrated probe.

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:06.820
For an amount, titrate: directly if it's clear

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.380
and fast, by back titration if it's a

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.940
slow solid, and with a meter if it's

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.340
coloured or cloudy.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.700
Then say what your result means.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.540
Next: analysing a household sample yourself.

00:07:20.767 --> 00:07:22.206
Here are the key notes.

00:07:22.447 --> 00:07:24.366
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:24.447 --> 00:07:25.246
to copy them.
